WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:12.340
TARIQ SHAUKAT: All right.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.100
Good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.730
I have, as Diane mentioned,
the coveted before-lunch spot.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:22.110
So thank you to Allison
and the organizers here.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.590
There's a study
out of Israel-- I

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.240
think this was published
five years ago, maybe four

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.910
years ago-- that looked
at parole rulings

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.960
by the time of the day and
basically showed that when

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.230
you get right before
the meal break--

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.669
right now, where the red arrow
is-- that basically you're just

00:00:41.669 --> 00:00:42.710
going to go back to jail.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.040
So you're not getting
a favorable ruling.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.080
So I'm, again,
deeply appreciative.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.330
Thank you, Allison.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:55.160
So with that said, I
will try and make sure

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.480
that we keep this
energized and interesting

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.197
and get you off to lunch
shortly thereafter.

00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:03.280
But as some of you know,
and Diane just mentioned,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.209
that I am a new hire at Google.

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:07.740
I came in about four
months ago, so I'm still I

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.680
think technically a Noogler.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.485
And I came from a traditional
brick and mortar company,

00:01:14.485 --> 00:01:19.064
a 77-, 78-year-old company,
Fortune 300 company,

00:01:19.064 --> 00:01:20.730
lots of different
properties everywhere,

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.050
lots of different lines of
business and a lot of mess,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.220
a lot of what one of
our customers refers

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.826
to as the "ugly enterprise,"
a lot of just stuff underneath

00:01:30.826 --> 00:01:32.200
the covers when
you looked at IT.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.890
And there are some examples
up here of that messiness.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.630
Legacy applications, in some
cases dating back 40 years,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.580
in some cases dating
back two months.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.780
This whole mix.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.640
Data silos literally everywhere.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.970
We had silos upon silos
and what have you.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.220
And really
importantly a changing

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.770
view on the value of data.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.620
There was some data that we
always knew was important.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.804
And there was other data--
video data, for example,

00:01:58.804 --> 00:02:00.470
we had no idea what
to do with the data.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:01.844
And it was expensive
to store it,

00:02:01.844 --> 00:02:04.499
so we sort of
threw the data out.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:05.290
We didn't store it.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.331
It just was purged
quite quickly.

00:02:09.331 --> 00:02:10.539
That, obviously, has changed.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:14.470
There's a lot more that you can
do with that range of data now.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.640
Regulatory environments, and I'm
sure a lot of you in the room

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.460
deal with on a daily basis,
bringing the regulators along

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.450
as you're trying to innovate.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.960
And then really
importantly, cloud skills

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.250
are really hard to find and
really hard to recruit for

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.460
and really hard to retain.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.020
And so this was our world.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.550
I expect that this is
a lot of your world.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.510
I see a lot of nodding
heads in the room.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.365
And by the way, as I've been
getting up to speed at Google,

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:42.240
as I've been meeting
with a lot of customers,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.110
I hear this from traditional
"ugly enterprises,"

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.110
from the traditional brick
and mortar companies,

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.980
and I hear this
from a lot of folks

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.530
that we think of as
start-ups that have actually

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.450
been evolving the way they
think about their architecture

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.250
as well.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.050
Now, the amazing thing for me,
and really what brought me here

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.060
to Google, was understanding
when you can even

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.470
start to make small dents
in these challenges,

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.550
when you can start to
pull out some of the data

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.710
and really apply some
real analytics to it,

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.340
some real machine
learning to it, et cetera,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.310
you can see amazing
impact on your business.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.430
We managed to bring
down costs, not

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:23.130
just IT cost but marketing cost,
sales cost, operations cost.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.590
We were able to grow revenues.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.050
We were able to really launch
products much more quickly.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.650
And it was all due
to using the cloud

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.780
to really unlock
the power that is

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.970
locked inside the organization.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.690
So as I come here to
Google, a large part of what

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.750
brought me here was this idea
of working with companies

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.580
and really helping them to
unlock the value that is hidden

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.470
within their companies that
is just sort of trapped

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.350
within these silos.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.230
As I've been focusing on what
we call our customer team

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.070
and really thinking
about, how do we

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:59.740
work with our customers to
really maximize their success?

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.670
There's three areas,
three pillars,

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.190
that we are focusing on.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.620
The first is investing
in your success.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.480
The second, which Urs and
others alluded to earlier today,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.340
is around partnership economics.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.940
And the third is taking the
partnership beyond support

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.415
into a whole new realm.

00:04:17.415 --> 00:04:19.540
And I'd just like to spend
a couple minutes talking

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.810
about each of these
different pieces,

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.820
starting with investing
in your success.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.980
I think Tim from SNAP said
it just a couple of minutes

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.360
ago that when you
move into the cloud

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.950
there's a lot of trust involved.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:35.780
There's a lot of trust involved.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:36.750
It's a partnership.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.330
It's not a quarter
by quarter thing.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.730
It is something that
really extends much longer

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.630
than that, years and decades.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:49.060
And we at Google are really
focusing on that interface

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.680
that we've got with customers.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.830
And our customer team, we're
hiring over 1,000 people

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.710
to join the customer team
to really sit and work

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.150
with customers to make sure that
it isn't a remote relationship

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:04.280
but it's something that is
really a personal relationship,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.010
because it is a
trust-based endeavor.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.500
Now, really importantly
for us at Google,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.020
as I've talked to customers
what they've really said is,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.280
help me unlock the power of
Google within my company.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.010
Help me get my engineers
connected to your engineers.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.360
And so as you look at
even our customer team,

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.310
we're actually focused on hiring
three times as many people

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.920
in the technical side as
in the non-technical side,

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.130
because the problem
solving that comes

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.200
when you can get engineers
connected with other engineers

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.159
and really start
to figure out, what

00:05:36.159 --> 00:05:37.450
should the workflows look like?

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.320
What should the
applications look like?

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:40.717
How do we migrate?

00:05:40.717 --> 00:05:42.300
How do we get from
point A to point B?

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.925
Well, that is really
powerful, and it's

00:05:43.925 --> 00:05:45.780
really dependent on
having people who

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.030
want to work with customers.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.530
And by the way, all
of our engineers

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.030
are dying to work
with customers.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.600
And we're hiring more and
more who want to do this,

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.224
getting them to actually sit
down and understand what's

00:05:55.224 --> 00:05:56.390
going on inside the company.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:57.320
It's not a new build.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.050
It's not a fresh start.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:03.530
So how do we help you get
from point A to point B?

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:04.760
So that's about scaling.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.510
The other thing that
we're doing is really

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.000
looking at the capabilities
that we're bringing to bear.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.170
And these are just a couple
of examples of changes

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.750
that we're making, of new
capabilities, new muscle,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.470
that we are building
within Google.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:21.360
The first is in the
office of the CTO.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.890
So one of the things
that I hear continuously

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:28.390
when I meet with CIOs
and CTOs is, you know,

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:29.640
I need help figuring this out.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.860
I don't just need help
with you explaining to me

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.290
what Google does.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:34.240
I need that as well.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.400
I need to understand
technically.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.816
I need to understand
the security.

00:06:36.816 --> 00:06:40.100
I need to understand the
pricing, all of that.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.960
But, really, I also need
help just understanding,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.440
how do I migrate?

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.770
How do I manage the board?

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.016
How do I manage our CEO?

00:06:48.016 --> 00:06:49.140
How do I deal with budgets?

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:51.470
How do I deal with
all of these issues?

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.055
So the idea of the
office of the CTO

00:06:53.055 --> 00:06:56.070
is to really bring peers
who are familiar with Google

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:59.440
but also familiar with what
it's like to work inside one

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:02.640
of these enterprises to be
advisers to really help you

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.520
with your migration.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.080
A second element here
is what we are referring

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.310
to as value optimization, or
the value optimization teams

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.240
at Google.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.630
And the idea behind
this is, again, it's

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:15.880
a trust-based partnership.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:17.590
It's a long-term partnership.

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:21.290
And it's one that is predicated
on us partnering with you

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:22.940
to make you successful.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.880
And so we've got teams
in our customer team

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.580
who are focused on
reducing your spend on GCP.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.450
Not by saying, please stop
using us or what have you,

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.610
but by actually helping to
make you more efficient,

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.160
helping where we see
opportunities for you

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.600
to save money moving on
to different services,

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.870
moving on, optimizing your
application, et cetera.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:43.840
Now, putting on my sales
hat, because I also

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.630
have a lot of sales people,
this isn't the best thing

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:48.300
for a given quarter.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.450
But we're not in this
for the given quarter.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.820
We're in this for the
long-term relationship

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.960
and really helping to
make you successful.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.069
And the last piece, which
I'll touch on later,

00:07:58.069 --> 00:07:59.110
is professional services.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:00.776
Now, we had Paul from
Accenture up here.

00:08:00.776 --> 00:08:04.050
I want to be very
clear that you will not

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.310
see 100,000 Google
professional services

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.760
folks running around
trying to serve customers.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.380
That's not what we're in the
personal services game for.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.450
What we are really
focusing on here

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.920
is enablement, both
customer enablement

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:17.960
and partner enablement.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.000
How do we actually
develop the IP?

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.580
It's one thing to
do it academically.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.621
It's the other thing to
do it with real customers

00:08:23.621 --> 00:08:25.640
and then bottle it up
and take it to partners

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:29.020
and help partners understand how
to actually go to market, how

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.320
to actually help customers.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.559
It's about training for
our customers developers,

00:08:34.559 --> 00:08:37.260
for our partners
developers, et cetera.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.799
So these are all new
skills and new capabilities

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:42.630
that we're really bringing
to market at scale.

00:08:45.339 --> 00:08:47.130
But getting to market
at scale is obviously

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.100
just one part of this.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.630
And Urs spent a
good amount of time

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.910
earlier talking about
in GCP how we've

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:54.760
been thinking about pricing.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:57.690
I sort of describe this
as partnership economics.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.800
This is something
where, as I mentioned,

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.280
our success is your
success and vice versa.

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:05.410
And we need to make
sure that we're actually

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:08.240
delivering our products
and services in a way that

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:10.190
demonstrates that partnership.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.160
Now, one of the
fascinating things

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.270
as I started this listening
tour, when I first joined,

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:21.850
is people are obviously
incredibly excited by cloud.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:23.710
This is a pretty full room.

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:26.430
Lots of people are just
interested in this concept.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:28.200
They see the idea.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:30.980
They see the potential that
can be a lot unlocked by moving

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:33.630
to cloud, and they're really
excited about how quickly

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:34.700
the world is moving.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:36.750
But you keep hearing
these "buts."

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.790
You keep hearing the--
but we're kind of worried

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.860
that the full
potential of the cloud

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.920
is not really being
delivered, particularly

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.049
on the economic side.

00:09:45.049 --> 00:09:46.590
We've gotten to a
place where I don't

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:48.540
have to own data
centers, which is great,

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:51.444
or, I can tap into services
on demand, which is great,

00:09:51.444 --> 00:09:53.360
but you're still sort
of making me figure out,

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:59.210
how do I plan capacity years
in advance, in many cases.

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.840
Elements like that, we
think, are under-delivering

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.060
on the promise of the cloud.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.860
And so you can look
at a lot of what

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.820
we're doing from a business
model and a pricing standpoint

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:10.100
under this umbrella
that Urs refers to

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:11.870
as "putting the
cloud back in cloud."

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.280
But really thinking
about, how do we

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.070
deliver not just technical
and the capabilities

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.239
that the cloud has promised
but also the economics

00:10:20.239 --> 00:10:22.280
and the business models
of the cloud as promised?

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.010
And you can see this-- I pulled
out some illustrative quotes

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.930
from conversations
with different CIOs.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.916
And you can see,
hopefully, some of these

00:10:34.916 --> 00:10:36.290
hopefully will
resonate with you.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:39.151
And we've got some
examples of the solutions

00:10:39.151 --> 00:10:40.900
that we've put in place
to try and address

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.050
some of these issues.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:45.130
And I won't read through
each one of them.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.540
I'll let you take a
quick look through them.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.440
But they're fundamentally
these questions

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.880
around, how do I
plan, how do I budget,

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.240
this idea of penalty
or punishment.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:55.990
All right?

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:58.100
Customers keep saying,
don't punish me

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:01.270
for actually being more
efficient about how

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:02.790
I'm using GCP.

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:04.740
Don't make me sign
up for something

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.140
that if I'm successful I will
end up missing and overpaying

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.830
for down the road.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:14.030
And so a lot of our effort
on the business side

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:15.670
has been in trying
to figure out,

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:18.920
how do we both deliver on
the flexibility of the cloud,

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:22.930
the on-demand of the cloud
reward, long-term commitments

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:25.000
and long-term
relationships, and also

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.280
take into account the
total cost of ownership?

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.360
We recognize this doesn't
stop at the doors of Google

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.950
or the doors of GCP, that
when you're looking at a cloud

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:37.787
migration you look at this
from the context of, how much

00:11:37.787 --> 00:11:38.370
am I spending?

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.280
How do I manage the
entirety of that budget?

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:43.860
There's no CFO that
I have ever met

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.530
who only cares about
this line item,

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:48.450
but you can do whatever
you want on the others.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.560
And so the focus that
we've talked about today

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.510
around automation, about
zero ops, around no ops,

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.170
is really meant to try and
address that skills gap

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:01.020
but also start to address the
overall total cost of ownership

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:03.600
question.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.260
And we believe that this works.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:09.040
It's not just solving
individual bit problems,

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.520
but when you add it together
what we see continuously

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:16.450
in competitive situation after
a competitive situation is

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.460
that this approach, the idea
of sustained-use discounts,

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:21.680
the idea of custom
VMs, et cetera,

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:27.840
can deliver between 20% to
30% lower pricing in aggregate

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.366
across the solution than
what you see from other cloud

00:12:30.366 --> 00:12:31.990
providers and,
obviously, substantially

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:35.500
lower than many on-prem
solutions as well.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:38.510
So we're excited by
this because we, again,

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.920
think that this not only
is the right thing to do

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.560
by the customer and helps us
build trust with the customer,

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.050
but we believe that it's
the right way for people

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:47.720
to start migrating
to the cloud and it's

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.480
the right expectation
that people

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.690
should have from the cloud.

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:57.070
Now, all of this is good if we
can build the relationships,

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:59.280
hopefully have you view
this as a partnership,

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.870
hopefully deliver the
ROI and the economics,

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:06.010
not make CFOs hate the cloud,
make CEOs very comfortable

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.800
with the fact that
they're operating

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.770
in a predictable environment,
in a environment in which we've

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.080
got each other's backs.

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.450
But as we looked at
that whole equation

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:19.070
and how we deal with customers,
we took a step back and said,

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:20.460
OK, what about that last part?

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.392
You've deployed.

00:13:21.392 --> 00:13:22.850
You're in the
process of deploying.

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:25.770
You've actually deployed
something in the cloud.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:27.340
You've taken a lot of risk.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.820
Now your business is
running somewhere else.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.430
How should we be
thinking about support?

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:35.580
Support, fundamentally,
is historically

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.682
quite a reactive function.

00:13:38.682 --> 00:13:40.420
If something breaks,
we will fix it.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.510
If something breaks, call the
call center, send the e-mail,

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.320
do whatever it is that
you do for support,

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.880
and we will dig into the
problem and we will help fix it.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.760
And obviously, that's important.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:51.440
If something breaks
we want it up

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.790
and running as
quickly as possible.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:58.340
What we're trying to do is to
take that concept of support

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.760
and really move it to
be beyond the reactive,

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.660
to be much more proactive about
helping to prevent problems

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:07.930
from actually happening
in the first place.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:10.090
And there's a couple of
different elements of this.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:13.460
I've talked about professional
services and training.

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:16.790
But it's hard to overstate
the emphasis that we're

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:19.600
putting on training
and certification

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.120
and really supporting
our customers

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:23.850
as they make this journey.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:27.770
So today we're announcing
two new certifications

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.060
that we've launched.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:34.240
One for cloud architects
to develop and recognize

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:36.240
professionals who spend
their time working

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.910
on developing and building
applications in the cloud.

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:42.080
The second, which we believe
is an industry first,

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:44.590
is around data
engineer certification.

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.660
How do you actually
build data solutions?

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:48.350
How do you build ML solutions?

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:51.640
Who's really been trained and
vetted to be able to do that?

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:54.430
So we're excited to
launch those pieces.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:58.430
And then we recognize
that the one side of this

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.860
is certifying people in advance.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:03.490
The other part is people
like to talk to people.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:05.799
The idea of, I
have a problem, let

00:15:05.799 --> 00:15:07.340
me find somebody
who can help me out.

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:09.620
Well, that, we think,
is really important.

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:12.240
So we're also creating
these environments

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:15.990
where you can get hands-on
dialogue with Google engineers,

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.880
not because you've got an
army of people walking around.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.500
We'll also help during
implementations.

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:23.280
But if you're just
working on some code

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.052
or you have a
machine-learning problem,

00:15:25.052 --> 00:15:26.760
and you've got a bunch
of data scientists

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:29.560
but you really want to
tap into a broader network

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:32.030
to help problem
solve more broadly,

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:35.050
we're creating this Advanced
Solutions Lab, which is really

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:36.630
focused on machine learning.

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:39.520
The idea behind this is that
our customers can work with us.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.260
They can send their
teams from our customers

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:46.120
to sit with Google
machine-learning experts,

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:49.020
and bring your own data
and let's collaboratively

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.220
help you solve that
problem, build the model,

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:52.741
tune the models, what have you.

00:15:52.741 --> 00:15:54.240
Whatever problem
that you're facing,

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:57.550
we can help take that
to the next level,

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:59.780
or at least be problem-solving
partners in that.

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:03.270
So that's what we're calling
the Advanced Solutions Lab.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:07.210
About two days ago-- actually, I
think exactly two days ago-- we

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:11.320
cut the ribbon on what we're
calling the Launch Pad.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.589
Launch Pad space here
in San Francisco--

00:16:13.589 --> 00:16:15.130
I don't know San
Francisco that well.

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:16.921
I'm told it's a couple
of blocks from here.

00:16:16.921 --> 00:16:19.420
If anyone wants to see it
we can certainly get you

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:21.320
the address later on today.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:24.380
But we did the ribbon-cutting
with the mayor.

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:27.250
Two days ago, this is a space
in which developers can just

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:30.830
come in and meet with other
Google Developers and say,

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:32.430
here's my code,
what do you think?

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:33.870
Here's my code, I'm
having this problem,

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.661
can you help me think
through this problem?

00:16:35.661 --> 00:16:37.350
How would I do this in GCP?

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:40.020
Questions like that that we
think just sort of starts

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:43.650
to foster not just the
formal interactions

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:45.645
but also gives a vehicle
for the informal.

00:16:45.645 --> 00:16:47.770
And we'll be looking as we
prove out the model here

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:51.190
to scale that more broadly.

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:54.530
Now, I'm really excited
to also introduce

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:57.610
this concept of Customer
Reliability Engineering.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:16:59.480
So we talked about
training and that

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:02.750
is all about helping your
developers, your partners,

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:05.869
get ahead of the applications
that you're building, again,

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:08.240
to ensure that we're
building reliability

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:09.869
into those applications.

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:14.214
But we realize fundamentally
that the applications

00:17:14.214 --> 00:17:15.839
that you're running,
the workloads that

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:18.991
you're moving to
the cloud, to GCP,

00:17:18.991 --> 00:17:20.740
it's one thing for us
to say, don't worry,

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.540
GCP will be up and running.

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:23.930
But if your app
goes down because

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:26.800
of something that happened
or some misunderstanding

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:32.370
of how to use GCP or what have
you, it doesn't feel very good.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:34.550
It doesn't feel very
good at all to say, well,

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:36.880
it wasn't our fault. It
was-- your application

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:37.890
was designed poorly.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.890
So the idea behind the
CREs, as we're calling them,

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:42.720
the Customer
Reliability Engineers,

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:46.600
is that they will actually
partner with the customer

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:48.860
and look at your
production applications.

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:51.897
And if a customer wants
it, dig into the code,

00:17:51.897 --> 00:17:53.730
put it through the same
production readiness

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:57.040
processes that Google applies
to its own applications that

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:58.970
also run in the cloud.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:03.780
So you can actually have our
team work with your team on,

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:08.890
is the application developed
right for the cloud?

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:10.790
Are your production
processes correct?

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:12.910
Is your operational
processes correct?

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:17.670
If you want, our pagers will
go off when your pagers go off.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:20.320
It really is a model of
shared accountability

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.390
that we are offering to take
for our customers' applications

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:25.500
running on GCP.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.480
Now, we've got a great
example of this after lunch

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:31.240
with Pokemon GO that
we'll talk about

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:34.510
and how they use CREs to really
get their service to where

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:35.010
it's been.

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:38.320
So I will-- we'll be talking
more about this later

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.070
on this afternoon.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:40.380
But we're very excited by this.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.080
We, again, think
this is something

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:43.600
that is unique in
the cloud world

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.650
and unique in the
engineering world.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.200
And we think that
this really changes

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:54.110
the equation of trust between
a provider-- a cloud provider

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.860
and a company.

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:59.380
And then finally, I'd
be remiss if I didn't

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:01.530
talk about our
partners and how we

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:05.090
are trying to enable
our partners' networks

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:10.150
to really focus on our customers
and help deliver solutions

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:11.950
with our customers.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:16.040
13,000 partners
are enrolled to--

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:19.514
and enlisted to help
people on Google Cloud.

00:19:19.514 --> 00:19:20.930
And you can see
the names on here.

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:24.860
They range from boutique
firms to the Accenture's,

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:26.570
who you saw up here,
PWC, who's been

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:29.990
a longtime partner of ours,
and the list goes on and on.

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:33.760
And we're very excited
by this ecosystem.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:37.022
We recognize that we
can't do everything,

00:19:37.022 --> 00:19:38.230
that you can't do everything.

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:39.896
And where you want a
partner-- we're not

00:19:39.896 --> 00:19:41.800
going to force you to
work with the partners,

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:43.925
but where you want the
partners, what we want to do

00:19:43.925 --> 00:19:46.996
is make sure that they are
trusted partners of Google,

00:19:46.996 --> 00:19:49.620
that they are trained in how to
use Google Cloud, that they are

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:51.860
trained to support
you in the right way,

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:54.120
that they're enabled
to get access to Google

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:57.320
so that you can enable
that engineer-to-engineer,

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:00.950
product-to-product engagement
that we've been talking about.

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:03.400
And in that spirit, we are
announcing two new partner

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:04.840
programs today as well.

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:06.750
The first is around competency.

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.445
So the same idea around
the certifications.

00:20:09.445 --> 00:20:11.290
We're really looking
at our partners

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:16.720
and saying that we can
certify that you have

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:18.932
a competency in certain areas.

00:20:18.932 --> 00:20:21.680
CINT here is an
example, who we have

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:24.380
certified as having a
competency in machine learning.

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:26.680
And that means that you
can trust with confidence

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.002
that Google believes that they
are great partners to work

00:20:30.002 --> 00:20:31.210
with you on machine learning.

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:35.400
We've got some more competencies
around app development and data

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.860
analytics, and
we'll be rolling out

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.120
more and more of
these over time.

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:42.500
And then, obviously,
I think Tim also

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:46.974
from STAT mentioned managed
services as really a big part

00:20:46.974 --> 00:20:48.140
of what's going on in cloud.

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:50.819
And we are continuing
to deepen our engagement

00:20:50.819 --> 00:20:52.110
with managed service providers.

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:54.750
And you can expect to see
more from us on this, both

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:58.440
in hybrid environments as
well as lift-and-shift types

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.670
of environments.

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:04.180
So with that, I spoke
quickly at the end

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:07.390
because I am starting to smell
the dinner-- the lunch that

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:09.750
is awaiting everybody outside.

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:12.175
So to recap, we've
got the three pillars

00:21:12.175 --> 00:21:13.800
that we've been
focusing on-- investing

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:16.420
in your success, the true
partnership economics,

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:18.990
and taking the relationship
beyond the support

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.002
relationship.

00:21:21.002 --> 00:21:21.960
So thank you very much.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:25.010
[APPLAUSE]

